ry uh momentous  event in the history of occupational  health um short waists were the words  that were used for for women's blouses  at the time  i've chosen this factory fire because it  illustrates this duality it was a  tragedy of huge proportions the  culmination of years of horrific working  conditions in a garment factory the  exploitation of immigrant women  in this case mostly women from eastern  europe  and nearly 150 preventable deaths were  caused when a fire started and the women  were unable to escape because the owners  had locked the fire exits  and so it's an illustration of how  harmful exposures are often associated  with disadvantage and exploitation how  physical stressors are often associated  with psychosocial stressors  and then on the flip side it's also the  story of how women organizing to  challenge the status quo and joining  labor unions were ultimately able to  galvanize international women's day in  response to this  tragedy  in 1962 biologist rachel carson  published silent spring identifying ddt  and other persistent pesticides as  harmful chemicals  and hugely raising awareness of  environmental toxicity  in 1976 an explosion near cevaso italy  led to massive community exposure to the  chemical dioxin  awareness of the potential risks coming  from such an explosion led to a  similarly massive study  the scientists in that area were able to  collect a lot of blood samples and they  set up a cohort study that's now been in  operation for nearly 40 years  and this study is one of the key mark  studies to document  both the role of endocrine disrupting  chemicals in the health of women and  then the children that they bear  as well as the nature of  general inheritance of environmental  toxicity  the 1980s saw the expanded use of a  large number of chemicals with unique  properties of persistence and also the  ability to mimic endocrine hormones  these chemicals such as teflon and  gore-tex  found their way into nearly every  product in our homes from the coating of  our furniture to its stuffing to  the food  that the the food packaging we used and  even our cookware  in the same decade  researchers noting earlier in earlier  age at menarche and declining sperm  counts in men  started raising questions about whether  these persistent chemicals could be  starting to alter the endocrine and  reproductive systems in humans  in the 1990s  the new stories shifted from humans to  amphibians and reptiles as frogs and  alligators striking malformations like  the one in this picture  these malformations were ultimately  linked to endocrine disrupting chemicals  released into waterways in the ambient  environment  the 2000s saw an expansion in the  scientific activities around endocrine  disrupting chemicals and reproductive  health  researchers documented that adult  diseases could be associated with  environmental exposures early in life  and even exposures to previous  generations  this led to something called the dohad  hypothesis or the developmental origins  of health and disease which now informs  environmental awareness in the realms of  maternal child health and environmental  health more broadly  and the 2000s also saw the expansion of  the cdc's biomonitoring program looking  for hundreds of chemicals in the blood  of americans  and unfortunately finding them in men  women and children  very frequently in some cases  surprisingly frequently  and then lastly  i highlight the recognition of the  importance of gender for understanding  the impacts of and also combating the  effects of climate change and  environmental challenges more broadly  and i picked the date of 2014 this is  a hallmark date in the un framework  convention on climate change which  monitors the global response to climate  change  when it adopted something called the  lima work program on gender  which requires that all countries take a  gendered perspective and look at the  role of gender in in the impacts of  climate change and also the solution  space  so the story of endocrine disrupting  chemicals starts with diethyl  stilbestral or des  to which millions of women were exposed  prior to the mid-1960s in the united  states in europe and also in australia  while thalidomide awakened physicians  and scientists to the reality that  fetuses are not quote unquote perfect  parasites protected completely by the  placenta but instead are actually  vulnerable to chemical insults  you'll recall that thalidomide led to an  epidemic of birth defects in in women in  the children of women who would use that  it was the des catastrophe that unfolded  in the 1960s and 70s that brought  greater awareness to the phenomenon of  endocrine disruption and the  multi-generational impacts of in-utero  exposure to female children in  particular  beyond the sentinel finding of clear  cell vaginal adenocarcinoma  children of des treated mothers suffered  a wide range of other structural changes  and reproductive problems  and yet despite this awareness des was  still given to livestock as a feed  additive until 1979.  you'll remember at the timeline i showed  mentioned biomonitoring  and here's just one graph demonstrating  how widespread exposures were  this graph is from 2004 and in this case  it's showing a population of pregnant  women  i mentioned some of these surpri some of  these findings were kind of surprising  and this would be one of them  the chemicals that are represented by  those blue orange green and purple bars  all have endocrine disrupting  potential they're chemicals like  organochlorine pesticides brominated  flame retardants polycyclic aromatic  hydrocarbons and phthalates and  alkyl perfluorinated alkyl substances or  phos  and you can see  even the least exposed woman  represented by that bar at the far left  of this graph  has well over  25 of these chemicals in her body  and even the most exposed woman in you  know  in in this set uh doesn't have that many  more so so the exposures to these  chemicals were common multiple and  pretty universal  most endocrine disrupting chemicals have  chemical structures that are very  similar to the body's own hormones and  this slide shows the many different  mechanisms by which they can disrupt  normal function  by mimicking sex hormones these  chemicals can either stimulate or block  hormone receptors  change a cell's signaling mechanisms  which leads to downstream kind of  effects  and in some cases even cause changes to  the expression of dna  through something called epigenetic  alterations and epigenetic alterations  are things like the methylation of the  dna molecule which affects its  expression and also changes to the  histone structures and the histones are  proteins that help to coil up or unravel  the dna and help to control its  expression as well  these epigenetic mechanisms can actually  these changes to to the methylation or  the histones can be inherited and  they're one of the ways that these  exposures can propagate health effects  across multiple generations  and this illustration illustrates this  the kind of transgenerational effects in  a more visual way  because the immature oocytes in a female  fetus which you can see there on that  right  picture  because those oocytes will ultimately  become the mature oocytes  when that fetus is born and becomes a  woman of reproductive age and then  ultimately  become that woman's child  any direct effects on those oocytes by  chemicals  that occur in utero  can manifest in the grandchild of the  exposed pregnant woman  and so the the grandmother to grandchild  effect can be a direct effect of  chemicals on the cells  within that fetus  in order to reach the great grandchild  or what's labeled as the f3 generation  there has to be some kind of heritable  change  such as the kind of changes i was just  mentioned and that are being  increasingly understood  through studying the epigenetic  alterations i just mentioned  most of the effects of des that we've  been talking about were observed through  the f2 generation or through the  grandchild  but as the f3 generation  is born grows and matures further study  may find more effects being inherited in  this fashion  and this table illustrates  the the multiplicity of effects of  endocrine disrupting chemicals and the  fact that different chemicals can cause  slightly different responses from  endocrine systems or in some cases  contrary responses making predictions of  how mixtures and cumulative exposures  will affect women and children very  complicated  so for example low molecular weight  phthalates the low  mwp on this table can lead to earlier  growth spurts while high molecular  weight phthalates have been shown to  delay growth  this complexity and mixture of effects  especially combined with the multiple  exposures that we've shown makes it  difficult to detect a signal  to detect population level changes in in  reproductive health or other kinds of  health impacts  but understanding  that on an individual basis  these chemicals can have potentially  serious consequences for reproductive  and overall health is leading to efforts  to reduce exposures to all endocrine  disruptors through a variety of  regulatory mechanisms  i'd like to move on now to considering  air pollution in women's health  while we've known for many years that  air pollution causes exacerbation of  lung diseases like asthma and copd  can cause heart disease and heart  attacks and premature death for both  sexes  more recent research has highlighted the  impacts of prenatal exposure to air  pollution on the developing brain and  other organs as well as documenting  adverse neurological impacts on women  towards the end of life  and this illustration shows some of the  mechanisms by which air pollution  affects the brain as well as some of its  other effects  um this includes uh and on the right  there you can see there's a number one  that that shows direct transport of  oxidizing air pollutants that may  deposit in the nasal passages and get  transported directly to the brain via  the olfactory nerves  the second is  the stimulation of inflammation cascades  and transport of inflammatory cytokines  from the lungs where those particles can  deposit  through this through the blood  circulation to the to harm the blood  vessels but also get into the brain by  that mechanism  and then the third mechanism that's  shown here is alterations of the gut  microbiome  recent  research is showing that air pollution  can alter the gut microbiome and we're  learning more and more how the gut  microbiome interacts with the brain and  can cause changes in in brain function  and structure as well  and then you can see in the effects  there that that find particulate matter  or pm 2.5  has also recently been shown to directly  affect women's fecundity lowering  ovarian reserve and new studies  so a wide range of health effects  for men women and children but some of  them unique to women's situation  and there's another aspect that's unique  to women's situation  which is uh the fact that  that  women are  predominantly exposed to household air  pollution  this is a problem of global scale and  it's related to the indoor combustion of  biofuels floating in heating among the  world's poorest populations  because most cultures place women in the  roles of caretaker  fuel gatherer and chief cook women and  their children are the most exposed to  household air pollution  leading to an enormous toll of lung  diseases and infections  heart and brain diseases and premature  death and you can see the quote there  from dr flavio bustrello who was the  assistant director general of the who  confirming that for women in low and  middle income countries household air  pollution is the single leading  environmental health risk  and so as we consider both endocrine  disrupting chemicals and air pollution  and the effects throughout different  lights life stages  the need to capture  as well as we possibly can  the totality of these exposures all of  these exposures throughout the life  course for research purposes starts to  become clear  a scientist named christopher wilde  coined the term expose zone 15 to 20  years ago to convey the same  comprehensive approach to understand  environmental exposures that researchers  were using when they talked about the  genome that ome genome to convey the  sense of the entire structure of our dna  this diagram just shows how measurements  of maternal and placental chemicals can  be used to capture the exposome of the  developing child  but the exposure very importantly is  more than just chemicals and air  pollutants  while i don't have time to go into all  of these exposures we know that many  other environmental factors from aspects  of our built environments like  walkability and green space  to lifestyles and behaviors to the  social and political environment that  leads to stress and constrained mobility  all of these can have enormous impacts  on our health both physical and mental  and importantly this slide also shows  that other aspects of our environment  including extreme weather events and  prolonged climate abnormalities  associated with climate change also have  profound effects on the health of all of  us  this slide about the impacts of climate  change on human health illustrates the  many different pathways and consequent  health outcomes by which climate change  affects our health and well-being  for each of these pathways women may  have unique exposures susceptibilities  and risks  i'll go into into a bit more detail on  just two of these pathways in a moment  first i just want to highlight the fact  that we  when we try to understand the impacts of  climate change on especially from a  gendered perspective we must take into  account contexts  of these climate change exposures  these include things like co-exposures  to air pollution and other chemicals  includes the constraints on men and  women by poverty often putting the  poverty lack of education discrimination  and other socio-economic factors which  may lead to disproportionate exposures  to a wide variety of environmental risks  and then a broader context of governance  land use  and urban or rural infrastructure among  other factors  all of these contexts  influence whether or not an individual  gets exposed whether or not they have a  harmful health effect from being exposed  and whether or not they're able to act  care or otherwise  be able to to be resilient to those  risks  and so this table which is adapted from  a paper written with colleagues from the  university of colorado in the united  kingdom  illustrates some of these factors in a  women's health perspective  women may experience unique or  disproportionate health risks from  extreme heat or climate related  disasters for several reasons  some reasons have to do with biological  factors that heighten risk  like a higher working metabolic rate or  reduced ability to sweat and cool makes  women in some cases more  susceptible to adverse consequences of  heat exposure  but other reasons have to do with  political or cultural factors and  discrimination  recognition of the importance of these  factors for women's health is a part of  the drive by the global organizations  combating climate change and its effects  force this gendered perspective and to  address the impacts on women subtly and  comprehensively  for example hiv aids is not generally  thought of as being a climate sensitive  disease and yet entered perspective  illustrates the connections between  climate change  drought  population displacement and the  vulnerability of young women hiv  as the story about this this study  that was published in post-medicine  demonstrates  and so having focused most of my talk  about the unique risks women face to  their health from environmental  exposures  i'd like to be returning to the start  and to honoring women's contributions to  bringing human society into greater  harmony with our planet environment  and i start with this quote from bandana  shiva a leading thinker in the role of  women in sustainability and  you can see she says that we're either  going to have a future where uh women  lead the way to make peace with the  earth  we're not going to have a human future  at all  next i want to recognize the continued  role of women organizing as they did  after the triangle short waste fire  to affect change  and this includes  groups in the united states like the  mom's clean air force and silent spring  institute which  in case of the clean air force obviously  deals with air pollution the silent  spring institute focuses its work on the  endocrine disrupting chemicals  as well as global groups like the  women's environment women's earth  alliance and the women's environment and  development organization  and i want to also  recognize some of the great individual  women leaders in environmental health  issues  that's rachel carson who published  silence spring next to her is christiana  fageres who was the the lead of the  un framework  convention on climate change helping to  address  the impacts of climate change and reduce  them  in the middle east group who wrote one  of the first  sentinel reports on sustainability with  her commission  and who later became the head of the  world health organization and worked on  women's health issues  next to her is gina mccarthy who was a  leading advocate for health as the  administrator of the environmental  protection agency and then to the right  is the next generation greta thunberg  who is clearly a woman  providing leadership in the  environmental arena  finally i want to acknowledge the  enormous importance of women's  empowerment more broadly in solving the  climate crisis  project drawdown which analyzed the top  100 measures to reduce greenhouse gas  emissions  has concluded that two aspects of women  in women's empowerment family planning  and educating girls  if you combine them they become the  single greatest step that can be taken  to reduce greenhouse gas emissions  and so we come full circle  as a woman's dance created a livable  world for humans  the uplifting of women around the world  is essential  to the survival of humans on the planet  i hope this talk has raised your  awareness and spurred many new thoughts  on the connections between our  environment and women's health  and i thank you for your attention  you

