nts on men and  women by poverty often putting the  poverty lack of education discrimination  and other socio-economic factors which  may lead to disproportionate exposures  to a wide variety of environmental risks  and then a broader context of governance  land use  and urban or rural infrastructure among  other factors  all of these contexts  influence whether or not an individual  gets exposed whether or not they have a  harmful health effect from being exposed  and whether or not they're able to act  care or otherwise  be able to to be resilient to those  risks  and so this table which is adapted from  a paper written with colleagues from the  university of colorado in the united  kingdom  illustrates some of these factors in a  women's health perspective  women may experience unique or  disproportionate health risks from  extreme heat or climate related  disasters for several reasons  some reasons have to do with biological  factors that heighten risk  like a higher working metabolic rate or  reduced ability to sweat and cool makes  women in some cases more  susceptible to adverse consequences of  heat exposure  but other reasons have to do with  political or cultural factors and  discrimination  recognition of the importance of these  factors for women's health is a part of  the drive by the global organizations  combating climate change and its effects  force this gendered perspective and to  address the impacts on women subtly and  comprehensively  for example hiv aids is not generally  thought of as being a climate sensitive  disease and yet entered perspective  illustrates the connections between  climate change  drought  population displacement and the  vulnerability of young women hiv  as the story about this this study  that was published in post-medicine  demonstrates  and so having focused most of my talk  about the unique risks women face to  their health from environmental  exposures  i'd like to be returning to the start  and to honoring women's contributions to  bringing human society into greater  harmony with our planet environment  and i start with this quote from bandana  shiva a leading thinker in the role of  women in sustainability and  you can see she says that we're either  going to have a future where uh women  lead the way to make peace with the  earth  we're not going to have a human future  at all  next i want to recognize the continued  role of women organizing as they did  after the triangle short waste fire  to affect change  and this includes  groups in the united states like the  mom's clean air force and silent spring  institute which  in case of the clean air force obviously  deals with air pollution the silent  spring institute focuses its work on the  endocrine disrupting chemicals  as well as global groups like the  women's environment women's earth  alliance and the women's environment and  development organization  and i want to also  recognize some of the great individual  women leaders in environmental health  issues  that's rachel carson who published  silence spring next to her is christiana  fageres who was the the lead of the  un framework  convention on climate change helping to  address  the impacts of climate change and reduce  them  in the middle east group who wrote one  of the first  sentinel reports on sustainability with  her commission  and who later became the head of the  world health organization and worked on  women's health issues  next to her is gina mccarthy who was a  leading advocate for health as the  administrator of the environmental  protection agency and then to the right  is the next generation greta thunberg  who is clearly a woman  providing leadership in the  environmental arena  finally i want to acknowledge the  enormous importance of women's  empowerment more broadly in solving the  climate crisis  project drawdown which analyzed the top  100 measures to reduce greenhouse gas  emissions  has concluded that two aspects of women  in women's empowerment family planning  and educating girls  if you combine them they become the  single greatest step that can be taken  to reduce greenhouse gas emissions  and so we come full circle  as a woman's dance created a livable  world for humans  the uplifting of women around the world  is essential  to the survival of humans on the planet  i hope this talk has raised your  awareness and spurred many new thoughts  on the connections between our  environment and women's health  and i thank you for your attention  you
 

